A||||5||jimmybpei||how to tell if slave cyl is bad HELP!!!||||1||
Z||000000||jimmybpei||10-26-2001||06:38 PM||||Need to know is slave cyl or master is bad.<P>After bleeding the cluth severla times i still hace no pressure on the peddle therefore not allowing the clutch to fully disengage, only nees about another 1/4 to 1/2  inch. <P>The car was an 86se 4cyl 5speed, now it is a caddy 4.5 5speed with only one exception i used a 6cyl cluth and pressure plate and still retained the 4cyl slave cyl. As far as i have read you can use any slave with any clutch ?????<P>any suggestions<P>Later Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||10-26-2001||07:11 PM||||Hey JimmyB  Measure your master cylinder travel down at the pedal.  It should be at least 1 1/4 inch.  The travel at the slave should be at least 3/4 to 1 inch.  We have found different diameter slaves and master cylinders.  At least if you measure you will know which end in causing the problem.  There are a few tricks that can be done to increase the throw if necessary.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nikotek||10-26-2001||07:26 PM||||Ive had this exact problem twice.Once was the master,the other time the slave. The slave is suspect if you have to pump the clutch many times to build the pressure to change gears. As told to me if you can pump the clutch which allows you to shift this indicates the slave bushings ang seals are not holding pressure. A quick way to fix this until you can replace the thing is to bend the clutch stop in(towards the firewall) which in-turn holds the pressure steady and doesn't allow air back in the system. Just remember to return the stop back to normal when slave is replaced...hope this helps a little.<P>------------------<BR>86' SE(308 kit)<BR>88' Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||jimmybpei||10-26-2001||08:16 PM||||Skitime - no need to measure anything because ive had the car driving when the four cyl was in it, all i had to do was bleeed it and i was on my way. thats why i posed the que? of the four cyl clutch and pplate. <P>Then i went and put a v6 clutch and pplate in with the caddy, thinking there is more surfacce area with the 6 cyl clutch and therefore better grab with no chance of slippage. ( the engine not overpowering the clutch - the 4cyl clutch that is)<P>The slve will only push the lever on the tranny all the way except for about a 1/4 to 1/2 inch. There is no pressure in the peddel at all either. <P>But the way just got the NOS for the yellow car yersterday.<BR>Also heading to Toronto on sunday to pick up GTBOY's rear fastback clip. got it for some cash and some good ol maritime beer.<P>Later <BR>Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jimmybpei||10-26-2001||08:20 PM||||nikotek - Do you mean at the peddel and bend that tab towards the firewall (front of the car)<P>Not sure i understand<BR>Thanks <BR>Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||10-27-2001||09:00 AM||||JimmyB  We were able to correct that problem by making a adjustable link between the master cylinder and the clutch pedal.  That removes all slack in the linkage.  Second we disassembled the master cylinder and ground about a 1/4 inch from the end of the plunger.  The end of the plunger has a thin rod extruding that prevents the plunger going in too far.  Removing a little of this increases the volume of fluid pushed. The net result was more slave travel and the clutch releasing.||||reg||1
